<article class="enc-article">
<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis Botha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1862">1862</a>–<a href="/years/1919">1919</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis Botha was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> general and statesman. He helped found the Nieuwe Republiek in 1884, rose to command the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a> forces in the Second Boer War and afterwards worked for reconciliation with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. Prime Minister of the self-governing Transvaal Colony from 1907, he became the first Prime Minister of the Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> in 1910 and led it into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, conquering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-south-west-africa">German South West Africa</a> in 1915.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-botha.jpg" alt="Louis Botha" width="154" height="216" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louisbotha_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Louis Botha was born on 27 September 1862. In 1884 he led Dinuzulu's Volunteers, a group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a> who had supported Dinuzulu against Zibhebhu, and in 1897 he became a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a> parliament for the district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vryheid">Vryheid</a>. In 1899 he joined the Krugersdorp Commando in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a>, fought under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucas-meyer">Lucas Meyer</a> in northern Natal and then as a general, leading Boer forces at Colenso and Spion Kop. After the death of P. J. Joubert he became commander-in-chief of the Transvaal Boers and fought at Belfast-Dalmanutha.</p><p>After a battle on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thukela-river">Tugela River</a> he granted General Buller a twenty-four-hour armistice to bury his dead. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a> wrote that Botha took him prisoner at the Battle of Chieveley on 15 November 1899. More recent research credits Field cornet Sarel Oosthuizen, and another account names the Italian Volunteer Legion under Camillo Ricchiardi. After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pretoria">Pretoria</a> in June 1900, Botha led a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> campaign with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koos-de-la-rey">Koos de la Rey</a> and Christiaan de Wet.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Botha represented the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a> at the peace negotiations of 1902 and signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-vereeniging">Treaty of Vereeniging</a>. In 1904 he and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-smuts">Jan Smuts</a> founded the Het Volk Party in the Transvaal Colony, which campaigned for self-government. After Het Volk won the elections of February 1907, Lord Selborne asked him to form a government, and he became prime minister on 4 March 1907. He took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-convention">National Convention</a> that led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa-act-1909">South Africa Act 1909</a>.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> became a dominion in 1910, Botha was its first prime minister. In 1911 he and Smuts formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-african-party">South African Party</a>. Widely viewed as too conciliatory towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, he faced revolts within his party and opposition from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/j-b-m-hertzog">J. B. M. Hertzog</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a>. Like Hertzog, he advocated the preservation of black traditions, which ultimately led to segregation. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> began he sent troops to take German South-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, a move unpopular among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a> that provoked the Boer Revolt.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> on 1 June 1919 Botha said that the peace made at Vereeniging had been bitter but not unjust. He thought the terms of the Versailles Treaty too harsh on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-powers">Central Powers</a>, yet signed it. He died of heart failure after an attack of Spanish influenza on 27 August 1919, aged 56, and was buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pretoria">Pretoria</a>. Equestrian statues of him stand before the South African Parliament building and the Union Buildings, and the General Botha Regiment bears his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Louis Botha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Men formed in the republic’s service, among them <b>Louis Botha</b> and Jan Smuts, went on to lead the Union of South Africa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/south-african-republic">South African Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>By the Treaty of Vereeniging in 1902 the two Boer republics became British colonies, and in 1906–1907 they received responsible government, which brought the Afrikaner generals <b>Louis Botha</b> and Jan Smuts to power in the Transvaal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/union-of-south-africa">Union of South Africa · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1884 Boers led by Lucas Meyer, among them the young <b>Louis Botha</b>, backed Dinuzulu, son of Cetshwayo, against Zibhebhu, and in return claimed a huge tract of Zulu land, where they proclaimed the Nieuwe Republiek with Lucas Meyer as president.</blockquote><a href="/state/nieuwe-republiek">Nieuwe Republiek</a></li><li><blockquote>Self-government came in 1907, and the Het Volk party of <b>Louis Botha</b> and Jan Smuts won the election.</blockquote><a href="/state/transvaal-colony">Transvaal Colony</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Greytown, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pretoria">Pretoria</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-29.07,30.58&amp;year=1899" aria-label="Greytown on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Louis Botha"><image href="/maps/globes/g030s30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5044 4920h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5044 4920h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-29.07,30.58&amp;year=1899">What was here in 1899? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
